<blockquote>
<p><strong>Bottom Line:</strong> Corpus Engine doesn&#39;t just find scriptures faster — it <strong>reduces your media workforce</strong>, <strong>eliminates human error</strong>, and <strong>enhances your broadcast productivity</strong> dramatically. One person can now do the work that used to require three.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr>
<h2>3. Getting Started: Registration &amp; Account Setup {#getting-started}</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Open Google Chrome</h3>
<p>Corpus Engine works best in <strong>Google Chrome</strong> browser. Open Chrome on your computer.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>⚠️ <strong>Important:</strong> We strongly recommend Google Chrome. Other browsers like Firefox or Safari may not support the live audio features properly.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Step 2: Go to the Corpus Engine Website</h3>
<p>Type this address into your browser:</p>
<pre><code>https://corpus.vidsyncapp.com
</code></pre>
<p>You will see the Corpus Engine landing page with a beautiful dark interface.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Create Your Account</h3>
<ol>
<li>Click the <strong>&quot;Start Your 7-Day Free Trial&quot;</strong> button (or <strong>&quot;Sign In&quot;</strong> if you already have an account)</li>
<li>Enter your <strong>email address</strong> and choose a <strong>password</strong></li>
<li>Check your email inbox for a <strong>verification link</strong> and click it</li>
<li>Once verified, you will be redirected back to Corpus Engine and logged in automatically</li>
</ol>
<h3>Step 4: You&#39;re In!</h3>
<p>After logging in, you will land on the <strong>Corpus Engine Dashboard</strong> — your central command centre for live broadcasts.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>💡 <strong>Cloud Sync:</strong> All your settings are saved to the cloud. This means if you log into Corpus Engine from a different computer (for example, the church desktop), all your Holyrics IP addresses, vMix settings, and preferences will already be there waiting for you.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr>
<h2>4. Understanding the Dashboard {#understanding-the-dashboard}</h2>
<p>When you log in, you will see the main dashboard split into three columns:</p>
<h3>Left Column: Live Transcript</h3>
<p>This is where you see the <strong>real-time text</strong> of what the preacher is saying. As the microphone picks up audio, spoken words appear here live.</p>
<h3>Centre Column: Staging Queue</h3>
<p>This is the &quot;waiting room&quot; for content that Corpus Engine has detected. When it hears a scripture reference or a song, a <strong>card</strong> appears here. You can review the card and then click <strong>&quot;PUSH LIVE&quot;</strong> to send it to the broadcast screen. (Or, if Auto-Push is enabled, it goes straight to the screen automatically.)</p>
<h3>Right Column: Program Output</h3>
<p>This shows you a <strong>live preview</strong> of exactly what is currently being displayed on your broadcast screen.</p>
<h3>Top Navigation Bar</h3>
<p>At the very top of the screen, you will see a row of toggle buttons:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Button</th>
<th>What It Does</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>📖 <strong>Bible</strong></td>
<td>Opens the full Bible browser (Ctrl + B)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>🎵 <strong>Lyrics</strong></td>
<td>Opens the Lyrics browser to push saved songs (Ctrl + L)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>▶ <strong>Start Session</strong></td>
<td>Begins recording the session for review later</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>🎙️ <strong>Audio: ON/OFF</strong></td>
<td>Turns the live microphone listening on or off</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>🤖 <strong>AI: ON/OFF</strong></td>
<td>Enables the AI engine for detecting paraphrased scriptures</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>📡 <strong>Captions: ON/OFF</strong></td>
<td>Enables Spoken Word Mode (live subtitles)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>🚀 <strong>Push: ON/OFF</strong></td>
<td>Enables Auto-Push (sends detected content straight to screen)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>⚙️ <strong>Settings</strong></td>
<td>Opens the full settings drawer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>🖥️ <strong>Launch Output</strong></td>
<td>Opens the output window on your projector/second monitor</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<hr>
<h2>5. Key Features Breakdown {#key-features}</h2>
<h3>⚡ Three-Tier Scripture Detection</h3>
<p>Corpus Engine uses three layers of intelligence to catch scriptures, each one faster than the last:</p>
<p><strong>Tier 1 — Lightning Regex (Instant, under 0.3 seconds)</strong>
When the preacher says an explicit reference like <em>&quot;John 3:16&quot;</em> or <em>&quot;Genesis chapter 1 verse 1&quot;</em>, the system intercepts it with a pattern-matching engine immediately. No AI needed.</p>
<p><strong>Tier 2 — Phrase-Based Search (~2 seconds)</strong>
When the preacher quotes a verse without naming it (e.g., <em>&quot;For God so loved the world...&quot;</em>), Corpus Engine searches the entire Bible for the matching phrase and displays it with the correct reference.</p>
<p><strong>Tier 3 — AI Semantic Fallback (~3 seconds)</strong>
When the preacher paraphrases a verse loosely (e.g., <em>&quot;That passage where Paul talks about running the race...&quot;</em>), the AI engine analyses the context and identifies the correct scripture (Hebrews 12:1).</p>
<h3>🎵 Automatic Song Lyrics</h3>
<p>When the worship team starts singing, Corpus Engine can detect the song title and automatically fetch the complete lyrics from the internet. The lyrics are broken into sections (Verse 1, Chorus, Bridge, etc.) so you can push them one section at a time.</p>
<h3>📖 Built-In Bible Browser</h3>
<p>Press <strong>Ctrl + B</strong> to open a full Bible browser. Select any book, chapter, and verse. Click a verse to push it live instantly.</p>
<h3>🎵 Lyrics Browser</h3>
<p>Press <strong>Ctrl + L</strong> to open the Lyrics browser. All songs you&#39;ve previously saved are listed here. Click a song to see its sections and push them live.</p>
<h3>🌐 Live Translation</h3>
<p>Enable translation in settings to automatically translate scriptures into another language (e.g., French, Yoruba, Spanish) and display both the original and translated text on screen.</p>
<h3>☁️ Cloud-Synced Settings</h3>
<p>Log in from any computer anywhere in the world — your settings, API keys, IP addresses, and preferences are all synced securely from the cloud.</p>
<hr>
<h2>6. PART A: Connecting Corpus Engine to Holyrics {#connecting-to-holyrics}</h2>
<p>This section walks you through <strong>every single step</strong> of connecting Corpus Engine to <strong>Holyrics</strong> so that when you push a scripture or lyric from Corpus Engine, it appears on the Holyrics projection screen automatically.</p>
<h3>Understanding the Connection</h3>
<p>Corpus Engine runs in your web browser (Chrome). Holyrics runs as a separate application on your computer. For them to talk to each other, Holyrics has a built-in &quot;API&quot; (think of it as a secret doorway) that allows other software to send it commands. We need to:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Turn on</strong> that doorway in Holyrics</li>
<li><strong>Get the keys</strong> (the Port number and Token)</li>
<li><strong>Give those keys</strong> to Corpus Engine in its Settings</li>
</ol>
<h3>Scenario A: Corpus Engine and Holyrics on the SAME Computer</h3>
<p>If both Corpus Engine (in your Chrome browser) and Holyrics are running on the <strong>same laptop or desktop</strong>, the IP address you will use is:</p>
<pre><code>127.0.0.1
</code></pre>
<p>This is a special address that means &quot;this computer&quot; (also called &quot;localhost&quot;).</p>
<h3>Scenario B: Corpus Engine and Holyrics on DIFFERENT Computers</h3>
<p>If Corpus Engine is running on Laptop A (for example, the media desk) and Holyrics is running on Laptop B (connected to the projector), you need to find the <strong>IP address of Laptop B</strong>. Here&#39;s how:</p>
<ol>
<li>On <strong>Laptop B</strong> (the one running Holyrics), press the <strong>Windows key + R</strong></li>
<li>Type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter</li>
<li>In the black Command Prompt window, type <code>ipconfig</code> and press Enter</li>
<li>Look for the line that says <strong>IPv4 Address</strong>. It will look something like <code>192.168.1.105</code></li>
<li>Write this number down — this is the IP address you will enter in Corpus Engine</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>⚠️ <strong>Important:</strong> Both computers MUST be connected to the <strong>same WiFi network</strong> for this to work. If Laptop A is on a different WiFi than Laptop B, they cannot communicate.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr>
<h3>Step-by-Step: Setting Up Holyrics API</h3>
<p><em>(If you do not have Holyrics installed yet, you can download it for free from <a href="https://holyrics.com.br/">https://holyrics.com.br/</a>)</em></p>
<h4>Step 1: Open Holyrics Settings</h4>
<ol>
<li>Open Holyrics on your computer</li>
<li>Click <strong>File</strong> in the top menu bar</li>
<li>Click <strong>Settings</strong> from the dropdown menu</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 2: Navigate to the API/Web Tab</h4>
<ol>
<li>In the Settings window that opens, look for a row of tabs along the left or top</li>
<li>Click on the tab labelled <strong>API/Web</strong> (or simply <strong>API</strong>)</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Edit</strong> button in this panel to unlock the settings.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 3: Enable the API and Get Your Credentials</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Check ALL the checkboxes</strong> you see in this panel (e.g., Enable API settings, Enable user HTP interfaces, etc.). This tells Holyrics to listen for external commands.</li>
<li>Look for the <strong>Port</strong> field — it usually shows a number like <strong>8091</strong>. Write this number down.</li>
<li>Look for the <strong>Token</strong> field — this is a long string of letters and numbers (like a password). <strong>Copy this token</strong> carefully (right-click → Copy, or Ctrl + C).</li>
</ol>
<p><img src="data:image/jpg;base64," alt="How to find the API settings in Holyrics"></p>
<ol start="4">
<li>Click <strong>Save</strong> or <strong>OK</strong> to close the settings</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> The Token is case-sensitive. Make sure you copy it exactly as shown, with no extra spaces before or after it.</p>
</blockquote>
<h4>Step 4: Enter the Credentials in Corpus Engine</h4>
<ol>
<li>Go back to Corpus Engine in your Chrome browser</li>
<li>Click the <strong>⚙️ Settings</strong> button (gear icon) in the top-right area</li>
<li>Scroll down until you find the <strong>Integrations</strong> section</li>
<li>Find the <strong>Holyrics Connection</strong> toggle and switch it <strong>ON</strong></li>
<li>Fill in the following fields:</li>
</ol>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>What to Enter</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>IP Address</strong></td>
<td><code>127.0.0.1</code> (if same computer) or the IP address you found earlier (e.g., <code>192.168.1.105</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Port</strong></td>
<td>The port number from Holyrics (usually <code>8091</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Token</strong></td>
<td>The token string you copied from Holyrics</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<ol start="6">
<li>Click <strong>Save Settings</strong></li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 5: Test the Connection &amp; View Communication Panel</h4>
<ol>
<li>Make sure Holyrics is running and a presentation is open</li>
<li>In Holyrics, look at the main screen and find the <strong>Communication Panel</strong> (usually on the right side or bottom, labelled &quot;Communication&quot; or &quot;Messages&quot;). This panel receives messages from Corpus Engine.</li>
<li>In Corpus Engine, say <em>&quot;John 3:16&quot;</em> into your microphone (or type it)</li>
<li>When the scripture card appears in the Staging Queue, click <strong>PUSH LIVE</strong></li>
<li><strong>Confirmation:</strong> The verse should instantly appear in the Holyrics Communication Panel, and if a presentation is active, it will push directly to your projection screen!</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>✅ If it works, congratulations! Corpus Engine is now connected to Holyrics.</p>
</blockquote>
<h4>Common Holyrics Errors and Solutions</h4>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Error</th>
<th>Cause</th>
<th>Solution</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>&quot;Connection Refused&quot;</strong></td>
<td>The API is not enabled in Holyrics, or the wrong port was entered</td>
<td>Go back to Holyrics Settings → API/Web and make sure all boxes are checked. Double-check the port number.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>&quot;Unauthorized&quot;</strong></td>
<td>The token you entered in Corpus Engine doesn&#39;t match the one in Holyrics</td>
<td>Go back to Holyrics, copy the token again very carefully, and paste it into Corpus Engine settings. Make sure there are no extra spaces.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>&quot;Network Error&quot;</strong></td>
<td>The two computers are not on the same WiFi network</td>
<td>Connect both computers to the exact same WiFi network and try again.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<hr>
<h2>7. PART B: Connecting Corpus Engine to vMix {#connecting-to-vmix}</h2>
<p>This section covers the <strong>complete end-to-end process</strong> of connecting Corpus Engine to <strong>vMix</strong> so that pushed scriptures and lyrics appear directly in your vMix title graphics on the broadcast output.</p>
<h3>Understanding How vMix Integration Works</h3>
<p>Unlike Holyrics (which handles its own projector screen), vMix is a <strong>video production switcher</strong>. It uses &quot;Title&quot; inputs (also called &quot;Lower Thirds&quot;) that overlay text on top of your camera feeds. Corpus Engine sends the scripture text directly into a specific text field inside your vMix Title input.</p>
<p>Here&#39;s the overall flow:</p>
<p><img src="data:image/jpg;base64," alt="How Corpus Engine connects to Holyrics and vMix"></p>
<hr>
<h3>PHASE 1: Prepare vMix — Add a Title Input</h3>
<p>Before Corpus Engine can send text to vMix, vMix needs to have a <strong>Title Input</strong> ready to receive it.</p>
<h4>Step 1: Open vMix</h4>
<p>Launch vMix on the computer that handles your live broadcast video mixing.</p>
<h4>Step 2: Add a Title Input</h4>
<ol>
<li>Look at the very bottom of the vMix window</li>
<li>Click the <strong>&quot;Add Input&quot;</strong> button (it has a &quot;+&quot; icon)</li>
<li>From the menu that appears, click <strong>&quot;Title / GTXD&quot;</strong></li>
<li>A file browser window will open. Navigate to the vMix Titles folder (usually at <code>C:\Program Files (x86)\vMix\titles\GT\Lower Thirds\</code>)</li>
<li>Select a template you like (for example, <strong>Element Blue</strong>) and click <strong>Open</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>The Title input will now appear as a new input at the bottom of your vMix window (e.g., <strong>Input 1</strong>).</p>
<h4>Step 3: Note the Input Number</h4>
<p>Look at the <strong>number</strong> displayed on the input thumbnail at the bottom of vMix. This number (e.g., <strong>1</strong>, <strong>2</strong>, <strong>3</strong>) is what you will enter into Corpus Engine later.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>💡 <strong>Important:</strong> Use just the <strong>number</strong> (e.g., <code>1</code>), not the name (e.g., &quot;Title 1 - Element Blue.gtzip&quot;). The number works more reliably.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr>
<h3>PHASE 2: Configure the Title in GT Designer</h3>
<p>The default vMix titles have a fixed text box that cannot handle long scriptures. The text will get cut off. We need to fix this by enabling <strong>Auto Shrink</strong> and <strong>Word Wrap</strong>.</p>
<p><img src="data:image/jpg;base64," alt="vMix Title Setup Process"></p>
<h4>Step 1: Open the GT Title Designer</h4>
<ol>
<li>Press the <strong>Windows Start</strong> button on your keyboard</li>
<li>Type <strong>&quot;GT Title Designer&quot;</strong> in the search bar</li>
<li>Click on the application called <strong>vMix GT Title Designer</strong> (it has a blue &quot;GT&quot; icon)</li>
<li>This opens a completely separate design application (it looks like a simple graphic design tool)</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 2: Open Your Title Template</h4>
<ol>
<li>In the GT Title Designer, click <strong>File → Open</strong></li>
<li>Navigate to the same folder where vMix titles are stored (<code>C:\Program Files (x86)\vMix\titles\GT\Lower Thirds\</code>)</li>
<li>Open the same template you added to vMix (e.g., Element Blue)</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 3: Select the Headline Text Layer</h4>
<ol>
<li>On the <strong>right side</strong> of the GT Designer window, you will see a list of layers (Text, Headline, Description, etc.)</li>
<li>Click on the layer called <strong>&quot;Headline&quot;</strong> — this is the main text field where Corpus Engine will write the scripture text</li>
<li>When you click it, you should see the text box highlighted in the design area</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 4: Enable Auto Shrink and Word Wrap</h4>
<ol>
<li>With the <strong>Headline</strong> layer selected, look at the <strong>top menu ribbon</strong></li>
<li>Click the <strong>Home</strong> tab</li>
<li>In the Text section of the ribbon, find and <strong>check</strong> these two boxes:<ul>
<li>✅ <strong>Auto Shrink</strong> — This makes the text automatically get smaller if the verse is too long to fit</li>
<li>✅ <strong>Word Wrap</strong> — This makes long text wrap to the next line instead of running off the edge</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 5: Save Your Modified Title</h4>
<ol>
<li>Click <strong>File → Save As</strong></li>
<li>Give it a new name like <strong>&quot;Corpus Engine Title&quot;</strong> or <strong>&quot;Scripture Lower Third&quot;</strong></li>
<li>Save it in the same Titles folder</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 6: Add the Modified Title to vMix</h4>
<ol>
<li>Go back to your main vMix window</li>
<li>If you already have the old title, right-click it and select <strong>Remove</strong></li>
<li>Click <strong>Add Input → Title / GTXD</strong> again</li>
<li>This time, select your newly saved <strong>&quot;Corpus Engine Title&quot;</strong> file</li>
<li>It will appear as a new input. Note the input number.</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h3>PHASE 3: Enable the vMix Web Controller</h3>
<p>The vMix Web Controller is a built-in feature that allows other software (like Corpus Engine) to send commands to vMix over the network. It needs to be turned ON.</p>
<h4>Step 1: Open vMix Settings</h4>
<ol>
<li>In vMix, click <strong>Settings</strong> in the top-right corner of the main window</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 2: Enable the Web Controller</h4>
<ol>
<li>In the Settings window, click on the <strong>Web Controller</strong> tab</li>
<li>Make sure it says <strong>&quot;Running&quot;</strong> or there is an <strong>&quot;Enable&quot;</strong> button you can click</li>
<li>The default port is <strong>8088</strong>. Do not change this unless you have a specific reason to.</li>
<li>Click <strong>OK</strong> to save</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 3: Find the vMix Computer&#39;s IP Address (If on a Different Computer)</h4>
<p>If vMix is on a <strong>different computer</strong> from the one running Corpus Engine:</p>
<ol>
<li>On the <strong>vMix computer</strong>, press <strong>Windows key + R</strong></li>
<li>Type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter</li>
<li>Type <code>ipconfig</code> and press Enter</li>
<li>Find the <strong>IPv4 Address</strong> (e.g., <code>192.168.1.108</code>)</li>
<li>Write this down</li>
</ol>
<p>If vMix is on the <strong>same computer</strong> as Corpus Engine, use <code>127.0.0.1</code>.</p>
<hr>
<h3>PHASE 4: Configure Corpus Engine&#39;s vMix Settings</h3>
<h4>Step 1: Open Corpus Engine Settings</h4>
<ol>
<li>In Corpus Engine (in your Chrome browser), click the <strong>⚙️ Settings</strong> button</li>
<li>Scroll down to the <strong>Integrations</strong> section</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 2: Enable vMix and Enter Credentials</h4>
<ol>
<li>Find the <strong>vMix Connection</strong> toggle and switch it <strong>ON</strong></li>
<li>Fill in the following:</li>
</ol>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>What to Enter</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>IP Address</strong></td>
<td><code>127.0.0.1</code> (same computer) or the IPv4 address of the vMix computer (e.g., <code>192.168.1.108</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Input</strong></td>
<td>The input number of your Title in vMix (just the number, e.g., <code>1</code>)</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<ol start="3">
<li>Click <strong>Save Settings</strong></li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h3>PHASE 5: Test the vMix Connection</h3>
<ol>
<li>Make sure vMix is running and your Title input is visible</li>
<li>In Corpus Engine, type or say a scripture reference like <em>&quot;John 3:16&quot;</em></li>
<li>When the card appears in the Staging Queue, click <strong>PUSH LIVE</strong></li>
<li>The scripture text should instantly appear inside the Headline text field of your vMix Title!</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>✅ If the text appears in vMix, the connection is working perfectly.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr>
<h2>8. Browser Security Settings (CRITICAL) {#browser-security}</h2>
<h3>Why This Step is Necessary</h3>
<p>Corpus Engine runs on a <strong>secure cloud server</strong> (HTTPS). However, Holyrics and vMix run on your <strong>local computer</strong> or <strong>local network</strong> (HTTP — without the &quot;S&quot;). Modern web browsers like Chrome have a security rule called <strong>&quot;Mixed Content&quot;</strong> that automatically <strong>blocks</strong> secure websites from talking to insecure local devices.</p>
<p>This means that even if you enter all the correct IP addresses and tokens, <strong>Chrome will silently block the connection</strong> unless you explicitly tell it to allow this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>⚠️ <strong>This is the #1 reason why the connection fails.</strong> If nothing is happening in Holyrics or vMix when you push a verse, this is almost certainly the cause.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>How to Allow Insecure Content in Google Chrome</h3>
<p><img src="data:image/jpg;base64," alt="How to Allow Insecure Content in Chrome — Step by Step"></p>
<h4>Step 1: Click the Padlock Icon</h4>
<ol>
<li>Look at the <strong>address bar</strong> at the very top of Chrome (where the website URL is displayed)</li>
<li>On the <strong>far left</strong> of the address bar, you will see a small <strong>padlock icon</strong> (🔒) or a <strong>tune/slider icon</strong></li>
<li><strong>Click</strong> on that icon</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 2: Open Site Settings</h4>
<ol>
<li>A small dropdown menu will appear</li>
<li>Click on <strong>&quot;Site settings&quot;</strong> (it usually has a gear icon next to it)</li>
<li>A brand new Chrome tab will open showing all the permission settings for the Corpus Engine website</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 3: Change Insecure Content to &quot;Allow&quot;</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Scroll down</strong> the list of permissions until you find <strong>&quot;Insecure content&quot;</strong></li>
<li>Click the dropdown box next to it — it will say <strong>&quot;Block (default)&quot;</strong></li>
<li>Change it to <strong>&quot;Allow&quot;</strong></li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 4: Refresh the Page</h4>
<ol>
<li>Close the settings tab and go back to the Corpus Engine tab</li>
<li>Press <strong>Ctrl + Shift + R</strong> (hard refresh) to reload the page with the new permissions</li>
<li>Your vMix and Holyrics connections should now work!</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>⚠️ <strong>Note:</strong> You only need to do this once per browser. However, if Chrome updates or you clear your browser data, you may need to repeat this step.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>If You Are Using Brave Browser</h3>
<p>Brave has an additional security layer called &quot;Shields.&quot; To allow the connection:</p>
<ol>
<li>Click the <strong>Lion icon</strong> in the address bar</li>
<li>Toggle the switch to <strong>turn Shields DOWN</strong> for this site</li>
<li>Then also follow the Chrome steps above to allow Insecure Content</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h3>Optional: Installing a CORS Extension (For Holyrics on a Second Computer)</h3>
<p>If Holyrics is running on a <strong>separate computer</strong> and you encounter additional connection issues, you may need to install a <strong>CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) extension</strong> in Chrome. CORS is a browser security policy that prevents websites from making requests to different servers. The extension relaxes this rule.</p>
<h4>How to Install</h4>
<ol>
<li>Open Chrome and go to the Chrome Web Store: <code>https://chrome.google.com/webstore</code></li>
<li>Search for <strong>&quot;Allow CORS: Access-Control-Allow-Origin&quot;</strong></li>
<li>Click <strong>&quot;Add to Chrome&quot;</strong></li>
<li>Once installed, you will see a small icon in your Chrome toolbar (top right)</li>
<li>Click the icon and make sure it is <strong>toggled ON</strong> (it usually turns from grey to coloured)</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>💡 <strong>When is this needed?</strong> Only when Holyrics is on a different computer and Chrome is blocking the requests even after you&#39;ve allowed Insecure Content. For vMix, the Insecure Content setting alone is usually sufficient.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr>
<h2>9. Going Live on Sunday Morning {#going-live}</h2>
<p>Here is your complete checklist for a successful live broadcast with Corpus Engine:</p>
<h3>Pre-Service Setup (15 minutes before)</h3>
